    "content": "LLM progress is often measured based on benchmark performance, such as AIME [21, 4], SWE-bench [5], or others [22–24], highlighting their proficiency in mathematics, writing code, or othercomplex tasks. These benchmarks often rely on arithmetic or knowledge-based questions, wheresuccess is determined by recalling facts or using similar examples from the training data to solve theproblems presented. However, many studies have examined the possibility of data contamination[4, 25, 26], raising concerns about the validity of the improvements we observe across models.Furthermore, these tasks offer less insight into the models’ planning abilities because they rarelyrequire a long-term strategy.",
